  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 916 PM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThere is a change on the horizon but not until we get at least \none more day of active and widespread convection. The difference\ntoday will be a shortwave trough that will move through and \nbring increased lift across the area. Everything else remains in\nplace...the moisture, the instability, all the things. As the \nwave moves through, convection will become more scattered to \nwidespread. As previously mentioned, storm motions should be \nsomewhat faster than seen recently. Despite that, localized \nflash flooding is possible, especially over vulnerable terrain \nand burn scars. Small hail, gusty winds, and lightning remain a \nconcern as well.\n\nBehind the aformentioned shortwave, dry air beings to filter \nback into the region. It will take a few days before residual \nmoisture is completely scrubbed from the atmosphere so we can\nexpect some convection Thursday and Friday with coverage \ndecreasing each day. As things dry out, the primary threat of \nthunderstorms will switch from heavy rainfall to gusty outflow \nwinds up to 50 mph. \n\nHigh pressure sets up over Utah this weekend bringing hot and \nmostly dry conditions. Temperatures will run around 10 degrees \nabove normal, but with less moisture in the air, you can expect \nmore of a \"dry heat\" compared to what we've endured lately.",
